Vulkan: a multisampled colour render target is resolved by its render pass (a resolve attachment), and a view's
BGFX_CLEAR_DISCARD_* is honoured for the whole render pass it ends.

bgfx stored every multisampled colour attachment and resolved it after the pass with vkCmdResolveImage, which
reads all the samples back. On a tile GPU (Adreno, Mali, PowerVR) that is the full multisampled picture written to
memory and read again each frame: the 4x scene and the UI's 2x display-size layers. The single-sample image of
such a texture is now a resolve attachment of the frame buffer (VkSubpassDescription::pResolveAttachments): the
resolve happens on chip, and the samples are not written at all when the view that ends the pass discards them
(store op DONT_CARE). FrameBufferVK::resolve() then copies nothing (TextureVK::m_resolvedInPass); the mips of an
auto-mip target are still made. Not for a view in another format (sRGB: resolveRenderPass converts) or a layered
attachment; a multisampled colour without a resolve attachment keeps its samples whatever the view discards.

bgfx keeps one render pass open through consecutive views into the same frame buffer and rect, and took its load
and store ops from the view that began it: a discard on the last of those views never reached the store op. The
pass now takes the discard flags of the last view it will run (looked ahead over the sorted draws: the same frame
buffer and rect, no blit before it; a compute dispatch inside ends the pass early, so nothing is discarded then).
